<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-hidden-order-representation" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>:ember: Hidden Order Representation</title>
  <introduction>
    <p>
      Many of the most profound ideas in science are rooted in taking on new perspectives to think about
      physical systems that already have a well-established conventional representation. The <em>big ideas</em>
      do not emerge from changes in the physical system themselves (nor the methods used to observe and measure them),
      but rather newfound understandings that reveal hidden structures in how nature functions.
    </p>
    <p>
      :ember: The terms, concepts, and frameworks discussed in this section are NOT conventional. Moreover, they are
      subject to change as the author fine-tunes her personal heuristics/formulations/definitions/etc. so they are not
      just self-consistent, but also consistent with respect to well-established conventions. Read section with caution.
    </p>
    <p>
      Thank you for your patience!
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <xi:include href="sub-conceptual-pipeline.ptx"/>
  <xi:include href="sub-structural-foundations.ptx"/>

</section>